        {
          "type": "points",
          "say": "Four details. Deep copy keeps a memo of objects already copied, so shared references stay shared inside the copy, and cycles don't recurse forever. Immutable atoms like ints and strings aren't copied at all. Classes can customize it, with dunder copy and dunder deepcopy. And some objects can't be copied meaningfully: files, sockets, locks.",
          "props": {
            "title": "Deep copy, <em>in detail</em>",
            "items": [
              {
                "at": "memo",
                "label": "Memo dict",
                "title": "Shared stays shared",
                "body": "Each object copied once; cycles are safe.",
                "code": "memo[id(x)]"
              },
              {
                "at": "Immutable",
                "label": "Atoms",
                "title": "Not copied",
                "body": "ints, strs, None are reused as-is.",
                "code": "x is copy(x)"
              },
              {
                "at": "customize",
                "label": "Hooks",
                "title": "__deepcopy__",
                "body": "Classes control their own copying.",
                "code": "__copy__"
              },
